  <div class="hl"><div class="t">Tech cracked while the Dow held up into NVDA week</div><div class="s">The surface looked quiet, but the real story was underneath: semiconductors and high-beta tech absorbed the pressure while the Dow and financials held up better. The cleanest institutional signal was a large NVDA downside-hedge complex into earnings week, reinforced by outsized semiconductor stock prints in MU, AMAT, MPWR, and NVDA itself.</div></div>
  <div class="tiles"><div class='tile'><div class='l'>SPY</div><div class='v'>763.46</div><div class='d down'>-0.29%</div></div><div class='tile'><div class='l'>VIX</div><div class='v'>15.89</div><div class='d flat'>CHG N/A</div></div><div class='tile'><div class='l'>WTI</div><div class='v'>85.13</div><div class='d down'>-2.22%</div></div><div class='tile'><div class='l'>BRENT</div><div class='v'>90.55</div><div class='d down'>-2.29%</div></div><div class='tile'><div class='l'>GOLD</div><div class='v'>4698.40</div><div class='d up'>+0.38%</div></div><div class='tile'><div class='l'>SPY PUT/CALL PREM</div><div class='v'>0.89</div><div class='d flat'>CALL-TILTED</div></div></div>
  <div class='sec'><h2>OPENING MARKET NARRATIVE</h2><p>Report date: <em>2026-08-24</em>. Generated after the session using market data available through approximately <em>20:00 UTC</em>.</p><p>The headline was simple: the tape looked calm at the index level, but underneath it was a semiconductor and high-beta reset. SPY finished nearly flat at <em>763.46</em> for <em>-0.29%</em> versus its <em>765.72</em> prior close, while the session context from futures and news showed the Dow holding up as Nasdaq-linked risk faded ahead of Nvidia earnings, inflation data, and Jackson Hole.</p><p>That split mattered. Lower oil and easing longer-dated yields should have helped growth, yet single-stock and sector flow still leaned defensive in key tech pockets, especially semis. When the surface says “quiet” but the internals say “de-risking,” you want to trust the internals.</p></div><div class='sec gold'><h2>PREVIOUS FLOW LEDGER — GRADING</h2><p>No LEDGER_IN was supplied, so prior-session rows could not be graded this run.</p><p>Ledger record: <em>0 confirmed / 0 rejected / 0 pending</em> · Open rows carried forward: <em>0</em> · Hit rate on resolved rows to date: n/a</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Previous ledger input</td><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Grading status</td><td>No prior NAW FLOW LEDGER supplied</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec'><h2>MARKET DASHBOARD</h2><p>Data notes: QQQ, IWM, SMH, VIX, and Bitcoin close-return fields were not exposed cleanly in the retrieved snapshots; ETF order counts, cancelled-trade totals, and excluded-premium aggregates were not exposed as session-level totals.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SPY</td><td>763.46, -0.29%</td></tr><tr><td>QQQ</td><td>last from intraday series 707.14 at open minute; daily return n/a</td></tr><tr><td>IWM</td><td>last from intraday series 299.38 at open minute; daily return n/a</td></tr><tr><td>SMH</td><td>last from intraday series 550.77 at open minute; daily return n/a</td></tr><tr><td>VIX</td><td>15.89, change n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Oil (WTI / Brent)</td><td>85.13 -2.22% / 90.55 -2.29%</td></tr><tr><td>Gold</td><td>4698.40 +0.38%</td></tr><tr><td>Bitcoin</td><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><td>10Y / 30Y yield</td><td>4.69 / 5.23, change n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Clean single-stock options orders</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>ETF orders</td><td>n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Dark-pool prints</td><td>25 / total notional n/a</td></tr><tr><td>Cancelled trades removed</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitrage / deep-ITM / multi-leg excluded</td><td>deep-ITM excluded by filter; multi-leg excluded by filter; aggregate premium n/a</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec gold'><h2>SECTOR PERFORMANCE</h2><p>Financials led, communication services held up, and energy faded despite only a modest options skew difference. The bigger tell was technology: XLK closed <em>-0.84%</em> even though call premium still slightly exceeded put premium, a classic flow-versus-price divergence that says dip buyers showed up but were not strong enough to win the session.</p><p>Industrials were cleaner on direction. XLI fell <em>-0.70%</em> and options premium leaned decisively defensive, with put premium at <em>7.76M</em> against just <em>0.59M</em> in calls.</p><div class='sub'>Flow-versus-price divergences, ranked by disagreement</div><ol class='naw'><li>XLK: price <em>-0.84%</em> with call premium <em>6.34M</em> above put premium <em>5.82M</em>.</li><li>XLE: price <em>-0.80%</em> with call premium <em>6.22M</em> above put premium <em>5.42M</em>.</li><li>SPY: price <em>-0.29%</em> with call premium <em>704.83M</em> above put premium <em>627.44M</em>.</li></ol><p>Sector-tide snapshots added context. Technology sector tide opened with heavy negative net volume and persistent negative net call premium early, while Financials showed steadily positive call-premium leadership through much of the sampled session. Energy and Industrials both showed more defensive swings as the day developed.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Sector ETF</th><th>Last</th><th>Daily Return</th><th>Call Prem vs Put Prem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>XLF</td><td>58.20</td><td class='pos'>+1.25%</td><td>15.44M vs 5.18M</td></tr><tr><td>XLC</td><td>112.34</td><td class='pos'>+0.84%</td><td>1.36M vs 0.05M</td></tr><tr><td>XLB</td><td>53.58</td><td class='pos'>+0.07%</td><td>1.88M vs 1.20M</td></tr><tr><td>XLE</td><td>63.13</td><td class='neg'>-0.80%</td><td>6.22M vs 5.42M</td></tr><tr><td>SPY</td><td>763.46</td><td class='neg'>-0.29%</td><td>704.83M vs 627.44M</td></tr><tr><td>XLI</td><td>178.99</td><td class='neg'>-0.70%</td><td>0.59M vs 7.76M</td></tr><tr><td>XLK</td><td>180.03</td><td class='neg'>-0.84%</td><td>6.34M vs 5.82M</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec'><h2>INSTITUTIONAL STOCK BUYING AND SELLING</h2><p>Signed stock-flow directionality was not exposed directly in the retrieved stock-trade data, so this ranking uses largest retrieved single-stock institutional print notional as the proxy. These are best treated as institutional repositioning or transfer activity unless direction is independently confirmed elsewhere.</p><p>The most important cluster sat in semis. MU, AMAT, MPWR, and NVDA all appeared in outsized stock prints on a day when the semiconductor complex was already under pressure, which strengthens the read that institutions were actively moving risk around the space rather than just watching it trade lower.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Ticker</th><th>Proxy Notional</th><th>Est. Shares</th><th>Price Action</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MU</td><td>434.03M</td><td>476,734</td><td class='neg'>-5.87%</td><td>largest retrieved stock print proxy</td></tr><tr><td>AMAT</td><td>288.15M</td><td>585,282</td><td>n/a</td><td>large regular-session cross</td></tr><tr><td>GOOGL</td><td>223.11M</td><td>639,000</td><td>n/a</td><td>late contingent cross</td></tr><tr><td>CTVA</td><td>183.06M</td><td>2,182,707</td><td>n/a</td><td>large sweep-linked stock print</td></tr><tr><td>XOM</td><td>172.70M</td><td>1,060,000</td><td>n/a</td><td>late qualified contingent trade</td></tr><tr><td>SAN</td><td>112.50M</td><td>7,673,686</td><td>n/a</td><td>ADR institutional block</td></tr><tr><td>MPWR</td><td>106.45M</td><td>80,870</td><td>n/a</td><td>high-price institutional print</td></tr><tr><td>NVDA</td><td>94.49M</td><td>450,000</td><td class='neg'>-2.91%</td><td>late contingent cross</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec gold'><h2>CLEAN OPTIONS-FLOW ANALYSIS</h2><p>The cleanest conclusion from single-stock options was not broad bullish speculation. It was selective downside protection in semis into a major catalyst week, with Nvidia the center of gravity.</p><div class='sub'>Top conviction observations</div><ol class='naw'><li>MCHP Dec 2026 65 calls printed <em>118,920</em> contracts for <em>153.41M</em> at <em>15:30 UTC</em>, with volume almost matching open interest. On size alone it dominates the day, but the execution and structure argue caution: deep-in-the-money, very high delta, and paired same-session activity in the Oct 72.5 calls suggest this may be stock-replacement, financing, or programmatic restructuring rather than clean directional call buying.</li><li>NVDA Jun 2027 140 puts printed roughly <em>119,994</em> contracts for <em>54.84M</em> just before <em>17:57 UTC</em>. Volume was about <em>4.66x</em> open interest, the trade came with earnings this week, and the strike sat far below spot at <em>209.62</em>. That profile fits tail-risk or disaster hedge better than a simple near-term bearish bet.</li><li>NVDA Jan 2027 180 puts printed in at least two large clips totaling more than <em>90M</em> in premium across the retrieved rows. Volume exceeded open interest on the repeated line, IV sat near <em>41%</em>, and the timing right before earnings makes this look like institutional protection rather than casual speculation.</li><li>META Aug 26 557.5 puts were small in dollar terms at <em>379,850</em> but huge versus open interest at <em>319.6x</em>. With only two days to expiry, this reads like event-risk hedging or tactical downside speculation rather than long-duration conviction.</li><li>Repeated-hits alerts showed additional watchlist names: COIN Sep 220 calls, INTC Sep 91 calls with zero prior OI, GLW Apr 2027 100 puts, and BE Aug 28 197.5 calls. Those are notable, but they were materially lower quality than the NVDA hedge complex and the huge MCHP line.</li></ol><p>By category, the day favored put buying and defensive overlays over clean call chasing. No high-quality bull spread, bear spread, or risk-reversal evidence was retrieved cleanly enough to elevate above the single-leg names, and multi-leg flow was excluded from this ranking by design.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Ticker</th><th>Contract</th><th>Premium</th><th>Vol/OI</th><th>Read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>MCHP</td><td>Dec 2026 65C</td><td>153.41M</td><td>1.00x</td><td>likely stock-replacement or overwrite, not clean upside</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>NVDA</td><td>Jun 2027 140P</td><td>54.84M</td><td>4.66x</td><td>large downside hedge / crash put</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>NVDA</td><td>Jan 2027 180P</td><td>46.08M</td><td>1.28x</td><td>earnings-week hedge</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>NVDA</td><td>Jan 2027 180P</td><td>30.76M</td><td>2.11x</td><td>same line item repeated, adds program feel</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>META</td><td>Aug 2026 557.5P</td><td>0.38M</td><td>319.60x</td><td>near-dated hedge/speculation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec'><h2>RELATED ACTIVITY AND THEMES</h2><p>Semiconductors were the dominant institutional theme. Stock prints in MU, AMAT, MPWR and NVDA lined up with heavy defensive options interest in NVDA and weak price action across the complex, which is exactly the kind of cross-asset agreement you want when deciding what really mattered.</p><p>The shared story was timing. With Nvidia earnings due on <em>2026-08-26</em>, institutions appeared to reduce or hedge semiconductor exposure rather than press fresh upside. The huge MCHP call activity muddies the tape, but because it looks more like stock-replacement or structure than clean upside aggression, it does not overturn the broader defensive read.</p><div class='star'><b>&#9733; TAKEAWAY &mdash;</b> The single most important trade of the session was the NVDA long-dated put program, especially the Jun 2027 <em>140P</em> and Jan 2027 <em>180P</em> lines, because they were large, repeated, and timed into the week’s biggest catalyst.</div><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Key names</th><th>Bias</th><th>Key takeaway</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Semiconductor de-risking</td><td>NVDA, MU, AMAT, MCHP, MPWR</td><td>Bearish/Hedged</td><td>NVDA long-dated put demand was the cleanest options signal</td></tr><tr><td>Defensive rotation</td><td>XLF, XLC</td><td>Bullish relative</td><td>financials and communication held up while tech wobbled</td></tr><tr><td>Event-risk setup</td><td>NVDA, META, BE</td><td>Volatility-driven</td><td>near-dated and earnings-week prints clustered around catalysts</td></tr><tr><td>Commodity relief vs growth stress</td><td>XLE, gold, yields</td><td>Mixed</td><td>falling oil and softer yields did not rescue semis</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec gold'><h2>DARK-POOL ANALYSIS</h2><p>The dark-pool tape was crowded, but not all blocks carry equal information. The highest-value prints were the ones with either extreme percent-of-ADV or obvious linkage to same-day options themes.</p><p>CTVA stood out on scale at roughly <em>45.35%</em> of 30-day average volume in a single retrieved print. SAN was next at about <em>42.56%</em> of ADV. Those are not ordinary tape cleanups.</p><p>In semis, MU and AMAT mattered most. MU showed multiple very large prints across the day, including a <em>434.03M</em> cross near the close, which suggests continued institutional repositioning in a name that also sold off sharply. NVDA’s late <em>94.49M</em> contingent prints were smaller by ADV but important because they arrived on the same day as the large protective put complex.</p><p>Dark-pool transactions here are unsigned. The safest language is institutional cross, transfer, or repositioning — not accumulation or distribution.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Ticker</th><th>Shares</th><th>Price</th><th>Notional</th><th>% ADV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MU</td><td>476,734</td><td>910.43</td><td>434.03M</td><td>1.21%</td></tr><tr><td>MU</td><td>342,332</td><td>966.78</td><td>330.96M</td><td>0.87%</td></tr><tr><td>AMAT</td><td>585,282</td><td>492.32</td><td>288.15M</td><td>7.48%</td></tr><tr><td>GOOGL</td><td>639,000</td><td>349.16</td><td>223.11M</td><td>2.21%</td></tr><tr><td>CTVA</td><td>2,182,707</td><td>83.87</td><td>183.06M</td><td>45.35%</td></tr><tr><td>XOM</td><td>1,060,000</td><td>162.93</td><td>172.70M</td><td>7.44%</td></tr><tr><td>SAN</td><td>7,673,686</td><td>14.66</td><td>112.50M</td><td>42.56%</td></tr><tr><td>MPWR</td><td>80,870</td><td>1316.28</td><td>106.45M</td><td>9.86%</td></tr><tr><td>NVDA</td><td>450,000</td><td>209.98</td><td>94.49M</td><td>0.39%</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class='sec'><h2>IMPORTANT PERCENTAGE-OF-ADV PRINTS</h2><p>If you rank by tape abnormality instead of just notional, CTVA and SAN jump the line. Their percent-of-ADV readings make them the most abnormal stock prints retrieved, while AMAT and MPWR carry more thematic value because they sit inside the semiconductor de-risking cluster.</p><p>The combination to remember is this: MU for size, AMAT and MPWR for semiconductor concentration, and CTVA and SAN for sheer abnormality versus normal trading volume.</p><div class='tblwrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Ticker</th><th>Company</th><th>% 30-Day ADV